# Learn page writing

Write for someone opening Champions for the first time. Clarity beats cleverness.

## Do

- Explain the idea in full sentences before any shorthand.
- Define a term the first time it appears (STAB, resist, hole, wincon).
- One idea per paragraph or beat. Say what it is, why it matters, what to do.
- Use concrete Pokémon and moves as examples, not as substitutes for the explanation.
- Link forward gently: “You’ll cover X in the Holes lesson” — never assume they already know it.

## Don’t

- Cryptic coach-speak or slogan chains (“Two names, one STAB”, “the third slot is the patch or the loss condition”).
- String fragments that only make sense if the reader already follows the meta.
- Cross-lesson jargon without a one-line gloss (“check-versus-counter”, “donated KO”, “clock”).
- Fake progression labels (“Beginners do X. Intermediate players do Y.”).
- Telegraphic fragments pretending to be punchy: “Immune. Then you Dance.”

## Shape

- Heading names the topic in plain language.
- Body teaches. Takeaway (optional) is one sentence they can remember.
- Prefer “what / why / what to do” over metaphors and in-jokes.
- If a sentence only works after reading three other lessons, rewrite it until it stands alone.
